  "not_simulated": [
    { "what": "body diode", "why": "The engine's nmos has none. With the gate at the source it does not conduct the datasheet's diode, whose VSD is 0.8 V typical at 0.44 A. A negative vds swaps the terminals, so the channel does conduct when the gate is far enough above the lower terminal. A level shifter uses that path. Add a diode from source to drain if a circuit needs the diode itself." },
    { "what": "drain and source resistance", "why": "The square law folds them in. At VGS = 10 V and ID = 0.22 A the model gives about 0.357 ohm, against the table's 0.7 ohm typical." },
    { "what": "subthreshold conduction", "why": "Below 1.3 V of Vgs the drain passes only the engine's 1e-12 S leakage." },
    { "what": "voltage-dependent capacitances", "why": "The capacitances are the typical values at VDS = 25 V, held constant, so gate charge is approximate." },
    { "what": "breakdown", "why": "VDS beyond 50 V and VGS beyond 20 V are not checked." },
    { "what": "temperature", "why": "The fit is at 25 C and the engine has no temperature." },
  ],
}
